 ## Background
-Chunk-based partitioning is a fundamental technique in high-performance computing and graphics, originating from the need to handle datasets exceeding single-machine memory limits. Early architectures like COLOSSUS utilized subvolume distribution across parallel units to achieve speedups, introducing concepts like boundary voxel replication to maintain visual continuity [S1]. The method has since evolved to support out-of-core streaming for interactive applications (e.g., voxel games), distributed MPI rendering for scientific visualization, and parallel training of neural radiance fields (NeRFs) for large-scale scenes.
+The concept of partitioning volumes into chunks for parallel processing dates back to early hardware architectures. The VOGUE system (1995) implemented a parallel architecture where datasets were divided into subvolumes distributed across different units, with ray properties passed between neighboring units [S1]. This approach required specific data distribution schemes to remove gaps between subvolumes by replicating boundary voxels [S1].
 
